# Development pipeline

@.tlk/PROJECT.md

---

## Kit Setup (for agents)

To set up talaka in a project, run from the **project root** (the directory that contains `talaka/`):

```bash
talaka/shared/lifecycle/tools/init.sh --non-interactive
```

Non-interactive flags: `-n`, `--non-interactive`, `--yes`, or `-y`. The script prints a **`[AGENT ACTION REQUIRED]`** block instead of spawning a nested CLI to edit `.tlk/PROJECT.md`.

**After the script exits, fill in `.tlk/PROJECT.md` yourself:** inspect the project files (`package.json`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `go.mod`, `Makefile`, version files, etc.) and replace every `<placeholder>` with the correct value. Then run `talaka/shared/project/tools/validate-config.sh` to confirm all placeholders are resolved.

**Layout — minimally invasive (the goal):**

```
project root/
├── .tlk/                          ← single home for kit-managed docs + state
│   ├── PIPELINE.md                ← canonical pipeline (kit-managed; refreshed on init)
│   ├── PROJECT.md                 ← project-specific config (you edit this)
│   ├── memory/                    ← layered memory (auto-managed)
│   ├── features/                  ← active feature folders (created per /requirements-eliciting)
│   ├── archive/                   ← completed features (moved by Zlydni)
│   └── scratch/                   ← ephemeral runtime files (auto-managed)
├── CLAUDE.md                      ← Claude Code entry-point with managed include block
├── AGENTS.md                      ← cross-IDE entry-point with the same include block
├── .claude/                       ← agent + skill copies
└── .gitignore                     ← managed block ignores .tlk/{memory,features,archive,scratch,…}
```

Everything kit-touched is either inside `.tlk/`, inside `.claude/`, or wrapped in a removable `<!-- talaka:start --> … <!-- talaka:end -->` block in `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` / `.gitignore`. `talaka/shared/lifecycle/tools/teardown.sh` either strips the block or removes the file when its SHA-256 still matches the kit copy.

**What `init.sh` produces:**

- Skills copied to `.claude/skills/`
- Agents copied to `.claude/agents/`
- Pipeline entry-point: managed include block in `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`, both pointing to `@.tlk/PIPELINE.md`

Any workspace-aware IDE discovers the kit through `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md` (the cross-IDE convention). One install covers all of them.

