## Pipeline Overview

A **coordinator-driven** team of agents. The coordinator — the main session the user talks to — is the only actor that invokes agents. Each agent does its own task, appends an entry to `handoff-log.md`, and returns. The coordinator reads the log and decides who runs next. Nothing ships without passing Bagnik.

```
Idea → requirements-eliciting (spec) → ux-designing (UX) + Mokash (docs, parallel)
     → mockups-creating (mockups) → User UAT
     → architecture-planning (arch + tests) → Bagnik (test gate)
     → Cmok (build) + Mokash (docs, parallel) → Bagnik (code QA)
     → Zlydni (commit + archive)

    [side-loop] Yaga — invoked ad-hoc on hard bugs:
       /bugs-diagnosing (hypothesis) → @yaga (instrument + observe) → @cmok (fix)
       → @bagnik (gate) → @yaga (strip instrumentation) → @zlydni
```

Every `→` above is a **coordinator decision**, not a call one agent makes to another. Agents never see each other; they see the coordinator.

### The one routing rule

> **No agent and no skill invokes another agent.**
> Do your task → append a return entry to `handoff-log.md` → return to the coordinator.
> The coordinator reads the log and decides who runs next.

Concretely, in every agent and skill prompt in this kit:

- **Never** call the Agent/Task tool, and never launch, spawn, or "auto-invoke" `@bagnik`, `@cmok`, `@mokash`, `@yaga`, `@zlydni`, `@veles`, or any `/skill`.
- **Never** wait on, poll for, or block on another agent's result. You cannot observe one — you have already returned by the time it runs.
- **Do** name a *recommended* next step in your return block. A recommendation is data for the coordinator, not an instruction it must obey.
- Mentioning another agent by name (in a recommendation, a fix note, or an artifact) is fine. Calling one is not.

**Why:** a single decision-maker holding the whole event log can see loops, repeated failures, and stalled gates that an agent — which only sees its own slice — cannot. Chained agents also nest context: each hop loses what the previous hop knew and the user loses the ability to intervene mid-chain.

